BT /F4 20 Tf 72 740 Td (Quiet Attention in Small Reading Groups) Tj ET
BT /F3 10 Tf 72 700 Td (A. Writer and B. Author) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 72 676 Td (Abstract) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 658 Td (Reading groups are a common way for small teams to keep up with a field, yet little is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 646 Td (known about how attention moves around the table during a session. This study followed) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 634 Td (nine informal groups over one term and recorded who spoke, who took notes, and which) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 622 Td (passages drew repeated comment. We found that attention clusters around a few dense) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 610 Td (paragraphs, that quiet members often return to those paragraphs later in writing, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 598 Td (that a short written prompt before the meeting spreads discussion more evenly. These) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 586 Td (results suggest simple, low-cost changes for anyone who runs a reading group.) Tj ET
BT /F2 10 Tf 72 566 Td (1. Introduction) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 548 Td (Most advice about reading groups is anecdotal. Organizers swap tips about snacks,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 536 Td (timing, and how to choose a paper, but few have looked closely at what actually happens) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 524 Td (once the discussion starts. Does everyone engage with the same parts of a text? Do the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 512 Td (loudest voices set the agenda, or do the quieter members steer the conversation in ways) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 500 Td (that are easy to miss? We wanted to answer these questions with careful observation) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 488 Td (rather than surveys, because people tend to remember a lively meeting as more balanced) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 476 Td (than it really was.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 456 Td (Our approach was deliberately modest. We did not record audio or video, and we did not) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 444 Td (ask anyone to change how they ran their meetings. Instead, a single observer sat in on) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 432 Td (each session with a seating chart and a simple coding sheet. Every time a member spoke,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 420 Td (the observer noted the time, the speaker, and the page or paragraph under discussion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 408 Td (After each meeting, members were invited to send a short note about anything they wished) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 396 Td (they had said. Those notes turned out to be the most revealing part of the data.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 376 Td (FIELD NOTES SUMMARY) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 358 Td (A recurring phrase in the notes was a quiet apology for not speaking up. One member) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 346 Td (wrote that the group had moved on before she found the words, and that the paragraph) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 334 Td (stayed with her all week.) Tj ET
