BT /F1 11 Tf 72 740 Td (3 Results) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 720.2 Td (Attention was far from even. In a typical session, roughly a fifth of the paragraphs in) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 708.2 Td (the assigned reading drew more than half of all comments. These were rarely the opening) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 696.2 Td (or closing sections; more often they were dense passages that introduced a new method or) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 684.2 Td (a surprising claim. Members who spoke least during the meeting were the most likely to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 672.2 Td (send a follow-up note, and their notes pointed to the same dense paragraphs about two) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 660.2 Td (thirds of the time. In other words, quiet members were paying attention to the right) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 648.2 Td (places but were not getting a chance to say so.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 628.2 Td (The written prompt helped. In groups that used it, the share of turns taken by the two) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 616.2 Td (most active members fell from about one half to about one third, and the number of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 604.2 Td (members who spoke at least once rose in almost every session. The comparison groups) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 592.2 Td (showed no such change. The effect appeared within two weeks and held steady for the rest) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 580.2 Td (of the term.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 560.2 Td (These findings come with limits. The groups were all volunteers, the observer could not) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 548.2 Td (be blind to which groups used the prompt, and one term is a short window. Still, the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 536.2 Td (pattern was consistent across groups of very different sizes, and the change asked of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 524.2 Td (organizers was tiny. A few minutes of writing before the meeting cost nothing and gave) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 72 512.2 Td (quieter members a way into the conversation.) Tj ET
